WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.960
A thread is a sequential
execution inside a process.

2
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.010
Because threads share the same address
space, they can pass data to each other

3
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:16.530
extremely fast. We can see this inside a
process running in user mode, operating

4
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.910
safely above the kernel's privilege boundary
using a shared area called a bounded

5
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.860
buffer. A classic way to move data
here is the producer-consumer model.

6
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.290
Producers generate data and place it into
the buffer, while consumers read and

7
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.160
process it. To coordinate,
they rely on two semaphores.

8
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.530
Before writing, a producer checks a semaphore
named empty, starting at the total number of

9
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.510
buffer cells. Before reading, a consumer
checks a semaphore named full, starting at

10
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.840
zero. If space is available,
the producer can write.

11
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.170
But to prevent a race condition when
updating the exact same slot, they apply a

12
00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.520
mutex, locking the shared
index for exclusive access.

13
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:59.330
When the work is done, the main program
can send a poison pill, a specific data

14
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.840
value flowing through the buffer, signaling
all threads to gracefully shut down.

15
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.540
This system works flawlessly because the
flow of resources is perfectly linear.

16
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.580
Data only moves in one direction.

17
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.150
But systems break down quickly when threads
need to lock multiple resources at the

18
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.760
exact same time. We can model this
problem using five dining philosophers.

19
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:21.900
They sit at a circular table.

20
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.230
Between each philosopher sits a single
chopstick, and every chopstick is protected

21
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.080
by a pthread_mutex_lock.

22
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.540
To eat their meal, a philosopher
needs two chopsticks.

23
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.050
In our system, this represents a thread
that requires exclusive access to two

24
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:40.120
different resources
simultaneously to do its job.

25
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.310
The process begins. Every philosopher
reaches out and successfully uses

26
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.660
pthread_mutex_lock to take
the chopstick on the right.

27
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.980
But when they turn to take the
left chopstick, they hit a wall.

28
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.900
It is already held by their neighbor.

29
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.680
Every philosopher goes to sleep waiting.

30
00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.750
The table freezes. This terminal output
shows exactly what happens when we run this

31
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.200
code on the course machine.

32
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.960
The threads completely lock up.

33
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.550
And after a three-second timeout, the system
kills the program, generating an exit

34
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.180
status of 124.

35
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.920
This specific failure
is called a deadlock.

36
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.240
It is a state where a set of
threads wait for each other forever.

37
00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.970
A deadlock does not happen randomly.

38
00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.890
It appears only if four strict rules, known
as the Coffman conditions, occur at the

39
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.720
same time. Condition
one is mutual exclusion.

40
00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.080
A chopstick can only be held
by one philosopher at a time.

41
00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:36.460
Condition two is hold and wait.

42
00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.660
A philosopher holds onto their first
chopstick while waiting for the second one.

43
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.040
Condition three is no preemption.

44
00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.170
The operating system cannot force a
philosopher to drop a chopstick they already

45
00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.900
hold. Condition four is a circular wait.

46
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.080
The dependencies form a
closed loop around the table.

47
00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.260
If all four of these conditions
are met, your system will deadlock.

48
00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.790
Because a deadlock requires all four
conditions, we can fix our program by breaking

49
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.230
just one of them. We will eliminate the
circular wait by assigning a number to every

50
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.220
chopstick. We introduce a global order.

51
00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.040
Always pick up the
lowest-numbered chopstick first.

52
00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.440
The final philosopher, between four
and zero, now reaches for zero.

53
00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.380
Since philosopher zero
holds it, they sleep.

54
00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.280
Chopstick four remains free.

55
00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.220
The neighbor takes it,
eats, and puts both down.

56
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.760
The loop is broken. You can break
deadlocks using other methods too.

57
00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.850
You can limit how many threads compete at
once by adding a waiter, or you can have

58
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.620
threads release their resources and
retry using the trylock function.

59
00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.780
A mutex alone cannot prevent a deadlock.

60
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.610
To write safe, concurrent C code, you must
architect the exact sequence in which

61
00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.440
your threads acquire their locks.

62
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.220
Let's review. Question one.

63
00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.500
In a bounded buffer, what do
the two semaphores wait on?

64
00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:56.540
They wait on the empty
slots and the full slots.

65
00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:01.290
Question two. Which Coffman condition is
broken by acquiring locks in a strict

66
00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.040
numerical order? The circular wait.

67
00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:10.750
Now, open animations A7.2 and A7.3
in the course hub, and run lab

68
00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.180
TD6.
